\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


A prime quadruplet is a tuple $(p,p+2,p+6,p+8)$ whose four entries are prime.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Are there infinitely many primes $p$ for which all four entries of this tuple are prime? \href{https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=List_of_unsolved_problems_in_mathematics&oldid=1366636767#Number_theory}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Do infinitely many primes start the four-prime pattern with offsets zero, two, six and eight?

